WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.374
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.052 --> 00:00:12.510
BRUNO AZIZA: Welcome to another
episode of "Data Journeys."

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.330
I'm Bruno Aziza, and I'm
excited to be here today

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.790
with Bob from Geotab.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.770
And so we're going
to learn from him

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.840
about his journey and the
amazing growth and success

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:23.340
he's had.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.100
And he's also going to share
with us his do's and his

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:26.790
don'ts.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.590
Bob, thank you so much
for taking the time

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:29.920
to talk to us today.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.900
Before we get started, tell
us a little bit about Geotab.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:33.900
BOB BRADLEY: Hey, Bruno.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:34.530
No problem.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.243
Yeah, so Geotab is a world
leader in the telematics space.

00:00:38.243 --> 00:00:39.660
And so really,
what that means is,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.560
we have millions of
connected vehicles.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.268
In fact, we have over
2 million connected

00:00:43.268 --> 00:00:46.380
vehicles driving around with
our little device inside of them

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.750
around the world, and a
really, really dense population

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.240
of vehicles here in North
America and a large population

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:52.630
in Europe, Latin America.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:54.690
And Geotab has been
expanding pretty rapidly

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.320
over the past few years.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.250
And so it gives us a really,
really unique look at the world

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.930
we live in, from a
data perspective,

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.850
because this data is immensely
useful for fleet managers.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.390
So they're using it to manage
where their vehicles go

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.650
and company operations.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.382
But on aggregate, these
2 million vehicles

00:01:10.382 --> 00:01:12.840
can tell us what the temperature
is on the side of the road

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.720
and whether a road is bumpy
in the middle of Europe

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.670
or in the middle of
the United States.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.848
And we can learn an awful
lot about cities around us

00:01:19.848 --> 00:01:21.390
and whether
intersections take longer

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:22.590
at certain times
of the day and what

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:23.800
traffic patterns look like.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.150
And so over the past five
years that I've been at Geotab,

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.790
we've gone from about 400,000
of these connected devices

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:33.060
to somewhere in the
2.2-million neighborhood today.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.640
And so that kind of growth
has been both amazing and a

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.570
challenge for us.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.028
BRUNO AZIZA: And now, the data
that you collect, of course,

00:01:39.028 --> 00:01:40.410
is 24/7.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.660
It's real-time.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.610
Tell us a little about
the volume there.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.902
BOB BRADLEY: Yeah, the data
we collect-- you're right--

00:01:46.902 --> 00:01:47.970
is real-time and 24/7.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:52.080
Customers of Geotab range from
small mom-and-pop trucking

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.940
companies to emergency services.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.760
So the data needs to
be correct and timely.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.430
And so we're streaming this
data in every single day.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.120
From a data and
analytics perspective,

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.210
that means about 40
billion points of data

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:04.960
that we get to play
with every single day.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.168
And I think we're somewhere
in the neighborhood of 11

00:02:07.168 --> 00:02:09.570
petabytes of data sitting
in Google BigQuery, which is

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:10.905
our data warehouse of choice.

00:02:10.905 --> 00:02:13.530
BRUNO AZIZA: So we're definitely
talking about large data sets,

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:14.730
fast-moving data sets.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.230
The products involved,
we're talking

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:20.430
about BigQuery, data flow,
real-time data, Cloud Composer.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.320
Tell us a little bit
about the use cases,

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.900
specifically, that you're
using these products for.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:25.650
BOB BRADLEY: Sure.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:29.550
So our customers are monitoring
their fleets, their own fleets,

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.260
looking at their own operations.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.620
But as a whole, if we can take
these 2.1 million vehicles,

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.260
and we can take this data
that's coming in at that pace,

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.360
and we can actually
run analytics on that,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:40.890
there's a lot we
can learn and teach

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.500
our customers about their data.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.250
So one of the use cases
that comes to mind

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.500
is, we had customers that
were telling us that battery

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.610
breakdowns were a big problem.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:50.460
And so we have fleets
that are one vehicle,

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.320
but we also have fleets that
might be 100,000 vehicles.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.830
And so if you scale up
roadside breakdowns,

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.820
the batteries themselves
aren't very expensive.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:00.150
But when you're seeing 80%
of our on-road breakdowns

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.820
happen because of electrical
systems, it's not the battery.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:03.570
It's not the part.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.100
It's the sending of a tow truck.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:05.730
It's the tow.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.130
It's the sending of a
mechanic and the replacing

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.690
of these parts that become
very, very expensive.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.060
And so we started a POC.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.872
One of the things that
I really believe in

00:03:13.872 --> 00:03:15.330
is getting customers
involved first

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.250
so we can really, truly
understand the customer

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:17.770
problem.

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:20.550
And so we thought, OK,
this particular customer

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:21.600
has a large fleet.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.800
But in the 2.1 million vehicles,
there's tens of thousands

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.140
of other vehicles
that drive and operate

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.240
in similar conditions
with similar engine types.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.060
What can we learn from
the group that we can then

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.190
help this customer
identify batteries

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.620
that potentially could fail?

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.980
And so we did a four-month POC
with a very, very large fleet.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:45.060
And the goal was to reduce
these roadside breakdowns.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:45.870
They're expensive.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:47.490
Product doesn't get to the door.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:48.510
SLAs are broken.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:51.420
And I think there were some
learnings here as well.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.820
I think our goal, on the
onset, was all to predict

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.123
a maintenance problem.

00:03:55.123 --> 00:03:56.790
Everybody talks about
mean time to fail.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.040
We need to tell this
customer, within three days,

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:00.422
your battery is going to fail.

00:04:00.422 --> 00:04:02.130
And what we learned
through the short POC

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:04.320
was that that really wasn't
what we needed to do.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.480
What we needed to do
was help this customer

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.440
with so many vehicles understand
which of their vehicles

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.900
are exhibiting
electrical-systems issues

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.960
and which of their vehicles
appear to have bad batteries

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.930
or don't look normal,
as it compares

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.209
to the rest of the 2.1
million vehicles that

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:22.480
operate like they do.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:25.080
And so it was a pretty
successful little POC

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.450
that has now spun
into a core product

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.660
that we can offer to customers
in that we were able to reduce

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.790
their downtime by--

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:37.260
almost 25% of the roadside
downtime was reduced.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.880
And there were little side
effects that we didn't expect.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.080
We had almost 10%
of less labor costs,

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.538
because there weren't so many
surprise battery replacements

00:04:45.538 --> 00:04:47.705
or electrical-systems tests
on the side of the road.

00:04:47.705 --> 00:04:49.110
So it was a great little POC.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.670
Of course, then the
problem becomes,

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:54.040
how do you scale from one
customer to many customers?

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.780
And I think, because we use
Google BigQuery and some

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:58.530
of those tools as our
core data warehouse,

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.905
and because that's where we
run a lot of our experiments,

00:05:00.905 --> 00:05:03.810
the scaling issue becomes
nonexistent for us

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:07.410
as we scale up and we
just lean in on some

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:09.510
of that capacity that
is offered to us by some

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:10.450
of those products.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:12.800
BRUNO AZIZA: And you have
been able to scale it,

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.810
as much innovation
as you put out here.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:18.457
Actually, I think you
have about eight patents

00:05:18.457 --> 00:05:19.290
on this particular--

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:20.580
BOB BRADLEY: Yeah.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:22.440
BRUNO AZIZA: --use
case, as an outcome.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.357
BOB BRADLEY: Yeah, there's
about eight patents

00:05:24.357 --> 00:05:26.670
on that particular use case
right now, as an outcome.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:31.200
And we're able to scale this
up and generalize the solution.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.780
And we'll be rolling it
out live with-- it actually

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:35.280
took us about a year.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.403
What makes us unique as
well, as a company, is,

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:38.820
since we manufacture
the hardware,

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:40.112
we're able to push out updates.

00:05:40.112 --> 00:05:43.140
So it became a real team effort
at Geotab in that we actually

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.718
discovered new, novel
ways of recording some

00:05:45.718 --> 00:05:47.760
of this information and
detecting changes to make

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:48.760
the product even better.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:50.385
So it was a bit of
an iterative process

00:05:50.385 --> 00:05:52.650
with the whole engineering
side of the company

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:53.700
to get this out there.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.312
But yeah, scale has
never been the problem.

00:05:56.312 --> 00:05:58.020
Ingesting the data
hasn't been a problem.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:00.437
And running queries over the
data and models over the data

00:06:00.437 --> 00:06:02.400
hasn't been a problem,
which really frees us

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.070
up, then, to work
hand-in-hand with customers

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:09.100
and really focusing on what the
actual mechanical problem is.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.100
BRUNO AZIZA: So that's
quite an amazing journey,

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.680
starting with one customer
and then expanding

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.320
that to the rest of your
fleet and understanding

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.200
there's a million data points
at a time and with the outcome,

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.660
with an amazing recognition
for your innovation.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.980
What have you learned
through the process here?

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.970
What are some of the
do's and don'ts here?

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.770
Maybe let's start with
the positive first.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.270
What are some of
the things that are

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:32.103
the do's that everybody
here listening to us

00:06:32.103 --> 00:06:33.180
should go out and do?

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.000
BOB BRADLEY: I think one thing
that taught us an awful lot--

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.310
and it had a waterfall
effect in lessons--

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:41.798
was engaging with customers
early in the process.

00:06:41.798 --> 00:06:43.590
I've been with Geotab
for about five years,

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:45.507
and the data team was
quite small at the time.

00:06:45.507 --> 00:06:46.710
And we've grown rapidly.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:50.640
And those first few years were
really learning and exploring

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.620
and playing with the
data and with customers

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.650
to understand the value that
we could then derive out

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:56.880
of it in the years to come.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:59.520
And so working with
customers closely and early

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.410
allowed us to start
conducting research

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:02.940
with a clear path to product.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:04.710
So we weren't
conducting research

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:06.750
for the sake of research,
but with a clear path,

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:08.400
like an applied
research approach

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.960
where the outcome should
solve a business problem.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.650
And really, the object
was to fail fast.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:18.180
The four-month POC was one
of many four-month POCs.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:19.530
And those other ones--

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:21.355
if we fail fast
and pivot, we can

00:07:21.355 --> 00:07:22.980
get to a solution a
little bit quicker.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:25.063
I think one of the biggest
learnings for any of us

00:07:25.063 --> 00:07:27.210
engineers-- we wanted
to make these 99%--

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:28.550
like I said, mean time to fail--

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:31.840
99%-accurate models, when,
at the end of the day,

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:35.040
we're saving real money
and saving real downtime

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.730
for customers without
needing to be 99% accurate.

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:39.540
So I think it was a
lesson learned there

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:43.170
that 99% accurate on a problem
that nobody wants you to solve

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:44.610
is not a good approach.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:47.670
Better to be reasonably accurate
on a large-scale problem that

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:50.520
can really help folks, and
then iterate on that problem

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:51.870
and bring up that accuracy.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:54.328
BRUNO AZIZA: Yeah, it's the
idea around being directionally

00:07:54.328 --> 00:07:56.100
right and then improving
on that results

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:59.520
rather than just trying
to be right and imperfect

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:00.750
rather than be done.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:02.490
What about the opposite of that?

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:04.680
What are some of the
mistakes that you

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:07.410
think people listening to us
should look out for and try

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:10.762
to avoid, even though they
are the result of things

00:08:10.762 --> 00:08:12.970
that might have been common
sense in the first place?

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:14.428
BOB BRADLEY: I've
said this before.

00:08:14.428 --> 00:08:16.320
I think I've said it
at some of your offices

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:17.437
doing a small talk.

00:08:17.437 --> 00:08:19.770
One of the best things about,
say, a product like Google

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:21.715
BigQuery is that
it's very, very easy

00:08:21.715 --> 00:08:24.090
to get your data into Google
BigQuery and move it around.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:25.770
And one of the worst things
about Google BigQuery

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:27.000
is that it's very,
very easy to get

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:28.413
your data into Google BigQuery.

00:08:28.413 --> 00:08:30.330
And one of the things
that Geotab did early on

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:32.255
was, we started
pushing as much data

00:08:32.255 --> 00:08:33.630
as we could into
Google BigQuery.

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:35.070
So it's not just
the vehicle data.

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.669
It's operational data.

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:39.120
It's all of our server
metrics, application logs.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.803
That really, really
helps us triage a problem

00:08:41.803 --> 00:08:42.720
from beginning to end.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.660
So definitely would not
change how we did that.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:49.980
But in doing so, you can't lag
data governance and DataOps

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.397
far behind, because you end up
with multiple sources of ground

00:08:53.397 --> 00:08:55.230
truth or disparate data
sets that are trying

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:56.500
to answer the same problem.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:59.970
And so I think with that ease
of use and ease of getting data

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:03.000
into BigQuery came some
hiccups along the way,

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.990
where, we need to now clean
up some of those resources,

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:07.740
and we need to figure
out which of A and B

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.460
is ground truth to
solve this problem.

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:12.235
So those data silos can
happen pretty quickly.

00:09:12.235 --> 00:09:14.235
BRUNO AZIZA: Yeah, and
they can cost you a lot--

00:09:14.235 --> 00:09:14.490
BOB BRADLEY: Yeah.

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:15.698
BRUNO AZIZA: --down the line.

00:09:15.698 --> 00:09:17.880
Did you have to hire
different types of folks

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:20.020
to make sure that
you'd managed for that?

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:22.530
How did you build
it into the process?

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:24.960
BOB BRADLEY: Yeah, over time,
I think we started out heavy

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:28.267
on the science and analyst
side of things, because we have

00:09:28.267 --> 00:09:30.600
so many managed services that
we leverage from you guys.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:34.440
But over time, we started to
pick up many, many more data

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.180
engineers and folks
that are focused

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:39.510
on pipelines and best practices,
data governance, and data

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:40.240
operations.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:42.810
So yeah, I think there was
a fundamental shift there.

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:44.670
What was amazing
about it, as I said,

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:46.985
those first few years
that I spent at Geotab

00:09:46.985 --> 00:09:49.110
were a lot of experimentation
and a lot of playing.

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:53.160
And we didn't have to worry
about the overhead of pipelines

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:54.040
and things like that.

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:56.283
But now as things
become mature products,

00:09:56.283 --> 00:09:58.200
we need to make sure
that those pipelines run,

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:01.390
that there's monitoring and SLAs
and all of those fun things.

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:03.708
And I think that we played
catch-up a little bit there.

00:10:03.708 --> 00:10:05.250
BRUNO AZIZA: That's
outstanding, Bob.

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:08.400
Thank you so much for sharing
these lessons with us today.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.610
I learned a lot about,
building better products means

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:13.132
engaging with customers early.

00:10:13.132 --> 00:10:15.090
And in your case, you
learned from one customer

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:17.140
and scaled it to
the rest of them.

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:18.630
So it's really quite amazing.

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:21.090
Eight patents, an
incredible story there.

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:23.250
And then the don't
is, don't wait

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:27.630
to implement DataOps and
data governance early, maybe

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:29.610
through data engineers
that you might

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:32.483
want to hire or a change in your
process, so you can avoid data

00:10:32.483 --> 00:10:33.900
silos, because
they could cost you

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:35.250
a lot throughout the journey.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:38.110
Again, like I said, thanks
for giving us the time today.

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:39.540
I hope many people are
going to reach out to you

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:40.415
and connect with you.

00:10:40.415 --> 00:10:42.330
They've got a lot to
learn from Geotab.

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.550
We appreciate your time,
and we appreciate you

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:46.260
being a great partner of ours.

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:47.218
We'll talk to you soon.

00:10:47.218 --> 00:10:48.218
BOB BRADLEY: No problem.

00:10:48.218 --> 00:10:48.840
Thanks, Bruno.

